WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:01.089
Good morning, John!

00:00:01.089 --> 00:00:03.230
I don't know if you've heard about this yet,
but there is a hip new challenge going on

00:00:03.230 --> 00:00:05.531
on YouTube right now and I am super interested
in it.

00:00:05.531 --> 00:00:09.590
It's called the "Explain the 2008 Financial
Crisis in Less Than Sixty Seconds" Challenge,

00:00:09.590 --> 00:00:10.660
so I'm gonna try to do it!

00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:13.740
Interest rates in 2008 were super low, which
meant that banks could basically borrow money

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:17.220
for free, so they had tons of potential money,
but they needed stuff to buy, and the most

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:20.411
important new thing to buy turned out to be
people's mortgages because there were more

00:00:20.411 --> 00:00:21.940
than thirteen trillion dollars of them.

00:00:21.940 --> 00:00:24.840
There are four institutions that are important
to this story: lenders who made the loans;

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:28.330
investment banks who bought, packaged, and
resold the loans; ratings agencies who told

00:00:28.330 --> 00:00:31.430
investors how risky the loans were; and investors
who bought the loans from the banks.

00:00:31.430 --> 00:00:33.910
Because of this new system, a bunch of people
did a bunch of things they should not have

00:00:33.910 --> 00:00:34.910
done.

00:00:34.910 --> 00:00:36.270
Lenders didn't need to worry about whether
the loans would be paid off anymore because

00:00:36.270 --> 00:00:39.210
they were just selling the loans off, and
because there was so much demand, they started

00:00:39.210 --> 00:00:42.590
to create manipulative loans that they gave
to people who could never pay them off.

00:00:42.590 --> 00:00:45.770
Those sub-prime loans were purchased by investment
banks who did a number of duplicitous things

00:00:45.770 --> 00:00:48.350
to make rating agencies think that they were
not sub-prime loans.

00:00:48.350 --> 00:00:51.460
They were rated as AAA and investors bought
them, thinking that they were getting a good

00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:53.970
thing, and eventually, when the inevitable
happened and people couldn't pay off their

00:00:53.970 --> 00:00:58.100
loans, the entire house of lies came crashing
down, along with a good portion of the global

00:00:58.100 --> 00:00:59.160
economy.

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:00.230
Wooooo!

00:01:00.230 --> 00:01:01.230
Challenge complete!

00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:03.300
And now I got like, three minutes left of
this video, so...

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:04.679
I should do some more challenges!?

00:01:04.679 --> 00:01:05.679
There's a bunch of good ones.

00:01:05.679 --> 00:01:07.289
The "Grow A Bunch of Hair" Challenge!

00:01:07.289 --> 00:01:12.950
Each of my 150,000 hair follicles produces
around .44 millimeters of hair per day, meaning

00:01:12.950 --> 00:01:18.649
that in total, I grow around 0.29 inches of
hair per second.

00:01:18.649 --> 00:01:23.179
Over the course of a day, if all of my hair
growth were concentrated on one hair, that

00:01:23.179 --> 00:01:26.630
hair would be 220 feet long!

00:01:26.630 --> 00:01:30.409
So while you can't see hair grow, you do grow
a lot of it.

00:01:30.409 --> 00:01:32.759
The "Try to Make A Harpsichord Noise Challenge".

00:01:32.759 --> 00:01:37.759
Bing gang ba-ding gang bwang gang bwang da-dang
ding dong tcka-tck ting tong tang.

00:01:37.759 --> 00:01:40.209
The "Drink However Much Mouthwash You Have
Left" Challenge.

00:01:40.209 --> 00:01:42.759
I should have bought a smaller bottle... ugh.

00:01:42.759 --> 00:01:45.460
